      "text": "To determine the probability that exactly two out of five 12-sided dice show an even number, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the probabilities of rolling an even or an odd number on a single die:**\n   - There are 6 faces numbered 1 to 6 that are even (2, 4, 6).\n   - There are 6 faces numbered 7 to 12 that are odd (7, 9, 11).\n   - Therefore, the probability that a single die shows an even number is \\( \\frac{6}{12} = \\frac{1}{2} \\).\n   - Similarly, the probability that a single die shows an odd number is also \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\).\n\n2. **Determine the number of ways to choose which 2 out of the 5 dice show even numbers:**\n   - This is a combination problem, specifically \\( \\binom{5}{2} \\), which represents the number of ways to choose 2 dice out of 5.\n   \\[\n   \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the probability of a specific combination where exactly 2 dice show even numbers and the other 3 dice show odd numbers:**\n   - The probability of a specific combination where 2 dice show even and 3 dice show odd is:\n   \\[\n   \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^2 \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^3 = \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^5 = \\frac{1}{32}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Combine the number of ways to choose the dice and the probability of each specific combination:**\n   - The total probability is the product of the number of ways to choose the dice and the probability of each specific combination:\n   \\[\n   10 \\times \\frac{1}{32} = \\frac{10}{32} = \\frac{5}{16}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that exactly two of the dice show an even number is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{5}{16}}\\).",
